<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- Focus in this system is one thing: a 2px halo of the accent at 30 percent, outside
         the border and never in place of one. Every control theme draws its own.

         Fluent draws a second one. Measured on 12.1.1: a keyboard focus puts a Border in
         the adorner layer, black at 2px with a #99ffffff border inside it, and the adorner
         layer is above everything a template draws, so it lands on top of our halo as a
         white square. With Fluent left out there is no adorner layer at all, so it is
         entirely Fluent's.

         Fluent is loaded on purpose, since it is what gives every type this library has
         not themed a template at all. This takes back the one thing it does that the
         design forbids.

         The cost is that a control we have not themed yet has no focus mark until it is
         themed. That is the right way round: a control with no look of ours is temporary,
         and a white square on a dark panel was never going to be right. -->

    <Style Selector=":is(Control)">
        <Setter Property="FocusAdorner" Value="{x:Null}" />
    </Style>

</Styles>
